1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,304
We get oxygen from the air we breathe, using the respiratory system. Air enters through the nose or mouth.

2
00:00:07,363 --> 00:00:12,121
It travels down the trachea, which divides into two bronchi, one for each lung.

3
00:00:12,121 --> 00:00:16,819
Inside the lungs, each bronchus branches into smaller tubes called bronchioles.

4
00:00:17,879 --> 00:00:21,090
Each bronchiole ends in tiny air sacs called alveoli.

5
00:00:21,090 --> 00:00:25,313
Below the lungs is a dome-shaped sheet of muscle called the diaphragm.

6
00:00:25,313 --> 00:00:29,713
Breathing is the physical process of moving air into and out of the lungs.

7
00:00:30,773 --> 00:00:37,136
Breathing in, or inhaling: the intercostal muscles between the ribs contract, pulling the ribs up and out.

8
00:00:37,136 --> 00:00:39,515
The diaphragm contracts and moves down.

9
00:00:39,515 --> 00:00:44,035
The chest gets bigger, the lungs expand, and the air pressure inside drops.

10
00:00:44,035 --> 00:00:46,889
Air from outside, at higher pressure, rushes in.

11
00:00:47,949 --> 00:00:53,242
Breathing out, or exhaling: the intercostal muscles relax and the ribs move down and in.

12
00:00:53,242 --> 00:00:55,680
The diaphragm relaxes and domes upwards.

13
00:00:55,680 --> 00:01:00,378
The chest gets smaller, the pressure in the lungs rises, and air is pushed out.

14
00:01:01,438 --> 00:01:04,768
Room air is pumped through one tube of clear limewater.

15
00:01:04,768 --> 00:01:08,693
A student gently breathes out through a straw into a second tube.

16
00:01:08,693 --> 00:01:12,559
Predict: which limewater will turn milky first? Pause and decide.

17
00:01:13,618 --> 00:01:16,830
Watch. Bubbles pass through both tubes for one minute.

18
00:01:17,889 --> 00:01:23,301
The limewater with breathed-out air turns milky quickly. The other is only slightly cloudy.

19
00:01:24,361 --> 00:01:28,226
Explain what you saw. Limewater turns milky with carbon dioxide.

20
00:01:28,226 --> 00:01:33,816
Breathed-out air contains about 4 percent carbon dioxide, a hundred times more than room air,

21
00:01:33,816 --> 00:01:39,347
because our cells make carbon dioxide in respiration. Pause and explain it in your own words.

22
00:01:40,407 --> 00:01:47,008
These are typical percentages of oxygen and carbon dioxide in the air we breathe in and the air we breathe out.

23
00:01:48,067 --> 00:01:51,992
The air we breathe out has less oxygen, 16 percent instead of 21,

24
00:01:51,992 --> 00:01:55,680
and much more carbon dioxide: about 4 percent instead of 0.04.

25
00:01:56,739 --> 00:02:02,686
Pause and decide: where did the missing oxygen go, and where did the extra carbon dioxide come from?

26
00:02:03,746 --> 00:02:07,552
The air we breathe out is different from the air we breathe in.

27
00:02:07,552 --> 00:02:14,034
It contains less oxygen, 16 percent instead of 21, and about a hundred times more carbon dioxide, 4 percent.

28
00:02:14,034 --> 00:02:18,019
It also has more water vapour: breathe on a mirror and it mists up!

29
00:02:19,078 --> 00:02:25,025
Gas exchange happens in the alveoli, which are surrounded by tiny blood vessels called capillaries.

30
00:02:25,025 --> 00:02:28,415
Oxygen moves from the air in the alveoli into the blood,

31
00:02:28,415 --> 00:02:32,994
and carbon dioxide moves from the blood into the alveoli, to be breathed out.

32
00:02:34,054 --> 00:02:39,822
The gases move by diffusion: from an area of high concentration to an area of low concentration.

33
00:02:39,822 --> 00:02:45,532
The walls of the alveoli and capillaries are only one cell thick, so gases pass through quickly.

34
00:02:46,591 --> 00:02:50,932
Breathing and respiration are different! Breathing moves air in and out.

35
00:02:50,932 --> 00:02:55,631
Respiration is a chemical process in our cells that releases energy from food.

36
00:02:55,631 --> 00:03:01,935
When it uses oxygen, it's called aerobic respiration: glucose plus oxygen gives carbon dioxide plus water.

37
00:03:02,994 --> 00:03:09,476
Starch from our food is digested into a sugar called glucose. In the mitochondria of plant and animal cells,

38
00:03:09,476 --> 00:03:15,959
a series of reactions releases its energy in a controlled, steady way, powering life processes all the time,

39
00:03:15,959 --> 00:03:22,322
even while we sleep. Germinating seeds respire too: the carbon dioxide they give off turns limewater milky.
